<div class="holiday-logic-info sitting-info">
    <p>Sitting dates for the <strong>federal Parliament</strong> are agreed to by each House and published a year at a time by the Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet, so coverage is limited to the years already published.</p>
    <p>Each year is split into the Autumn, Winter and Spring sitting blocks. Sittings held outside the published calendar are shown as <em>Recall</em>.</p>
    <p>Both chambers usually sit together, which the published calendar marks with an asterisk. Sitting together is not a joint sitting under section 57 of the Constitution. The exceptions are tagged <em>House only</em> or <em>Senate only</em>, for the periods one chamber sits without the other.</p>
    <p>Senate estimates are committee hearings rather than sittings, which is why the Senate has no sitting period during an estimates round while the House does.</p>
    <p>The calendar is indicative. Extra sittings can be added, scheduled ones cancelled, and the whole calendar is displaced when Parliament is dissolved for an election. Verify with the <a href="https://www.pmc.gov.au/resources/parliamentary-sittings" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official source</a> before relying on these dates.</p>
</div>
